Had a go at modifying the query.  

The original query had a rr=<timestamp> on the end, but I couldn't find this parameter in the nightscout source code, and didn't see it do too much when trying the queries directly with a web browser.

Modified the query to use the find parameter, based on the timestamp from the last BG reading.
        Call<List<Entry>> getEntries(@Header("api-secret") String secret, @Query("count") int count, @Query("find[date][$gte]") String rr);

Note that this changes the behaviour from querying the current time and retrieving a fixed number of samples to querying the most recent <count> samples since the given start time.  (A better change might be to take the most recent of (-24 hours, last BG reading timestamp).

Patched in this to adjust:
BgReading bgReading = BgReading.last(); 
long searchDate = bgReading != null ? bgReading.timestamp : (JoH.tsl() - (1000 * 60 * 60 * 24));
		
getService().getEntries(session.url.getHashedSecret(), count, searchDate + "").enqueue(session.entriesCallback);

(Basing this off MissedReadingEstimator - there will be a tidier way to do it)

Also, increased the count to a static count of (60*24), given that the date now bounds the search.

Net result of these changes was that I could see all of the data in the range being downloaded at startup (Checked with adb logcat), but only a reduced amount of data being displayed on the graph.

Had a quick look at app/src/main/java/com/eveningoutpost/dexdrip/UtilityModels/BgGraphBuilder.java, and tried changing this line:

    private static final int NUM_VALUES = (60 / 5) * 24;

to:

    private static final int NUM_VALUES = (60) * 24;

After this, the full graph showed up.

Will give these changes a try for a few days, see if anything else shows up.

Thank you for your efforts. I hope I'm not completely off the mark. But after looking at the PR, I suspect that can't be the solution. @AidasK is not using an Android Wear device, but a Garmin watch with a non Android firmware. This can only query bg values via the web service. Here, in the json response of the web service, the output would have to be reduced (and not extended).
For example, by changing line 164 in WebServiceSgv.java so that for minutely readings (Libre3) only every 5th a json record is produced:
```
                // for each reading produce a json record
                for (BgReading reading : readings) {
```

> @swissalpine Can you compile what you are suggesting and test it?

@Navid200 Thanks for coming back! I can compile it, but since I don't use a Libre sensor myself, a test is relatively useless.

Also many thanks to @jamorham, a filtering or a data reduction during import would also be a possibility.
